# CANONICAL earcut.hpp v2.2.4 SPECIFICATION (merged)

**Provenance note (important for trust weighting):** `/workspace/dataset/test4.cpp` and `/workspace/dataset/test4_executable` do exist on disk. The driver builds `n` rings, ring `i` = `[(2i,2i),(2i+n,2i),(2i+n,2i+n),(2i,2i+n)]` (CCW, side `n`, diagonal offset 2), ring 0 = outer, rings 1..n-1 = holes, and calls `mapbox::earcut<uint32_t>`. So Report 5's oracle outputs are real, and its variant verdicts (read from machine code) outrank the other four reports' recollections wherever they conflict — **except** where a claim contradicts three independent reports *and* is FP-neutral for this input (see Disagreement D2).

Derived facts for this input: total verts = `4n`; hashing turns on at **n ≥ 21**; every hole's leftmost vertex is `(2i,2i)` so all sort keys are distinct; all coordinates are small integers, so every product/difference in every predicate is **computed exactly** in `double` (no rounding at all until `n` gets large). That last fact demotes every "expression rearrangement" risk to near-zero for *this* input and promotes every *structural / comparison-operator* risk.

---

## PART 0 — Types and functor state

```
struct Node {
    const N      i;        // vertex index into flattened concatenation of all rings
    const double x, y;     // ALWAYS double, even for integer/float input points
    Node*        prev = nullptr;
    Node*        next = nullptr;
    int32_t      z    = 0;        // z-order (Morton) key
    Node*        prevZ = nullptr;
    Node*        nextZ = nullptr;
    bool         steiner = false;
};
// Rust: arena `Vec<Node>` + `u32`/`usize` handles. Every `p != q` in this spec is
// HANDLE identity, never coordinate comparison. Every `p->i != q->i` is INDEX
// comparison and is NOT the same thing (splitPolygon mints duplicate-index nodes).

struct Earcut<N> {
    std::vector<N>     indices;      // OUTPUT
    std::size_t        vertices = 0; // running base index; reset per call
    ObjectStore<Node>  nodes;        // arena; nodes are NEVER freed mid-run
    std::vector<Node*> queue;        // hole queue (member; cleared at entry — a local
                                     //   vector is behaviorally identical)
    bool   hashing;
    double minX, maxX, minY, maxY, inv_size;
};
```

Sign convention, load-bearing everywhere: `area()` is the **negative** of the usual CCW cross product. **Negative area == convex/CCW turn.** `area(...) >= 0` means reflex-or-collinear.

---

## PART 1 — Every function, in dependency order

### 1.1 `area`

```
double area(const Node* p, const Node* q, const Node* r) {
    return (q->y - p->y) * (r->x - q->x) - (q->x - p->x) * (r->y - q->y);
}
```
Verbatim. No rearrangement, no FMA (`-ffp-contract=off`), no x87 excess precision, no `-ffast-math`.

### 1.2 `equals`

```
bool equals(const Node* p1, const Node* p2) {
    return p1->x == p2->x && p1->y == p2->y;   // exact ==, no epsilon anywhere in earcut
}
```

### 1.3 `pointInTriangle`  *(see Disagreement D2)*

```
bool pointInTriangle(double ax, double ay, double bx, double by,
                     double cx, double cy, double px, double py) {
    return (cx - px) * (ay - py) - (ax - px) * (cy - py) >= 0 &&
           (ax - px) * (by - py) - (bx - px) * (ay - py) >= 0 &&
           (bx - px) * (cy - py) - (cx - px) * (by - py) >= 0;
}
```
Three cross products in the pair order (C,A), (A,B), (B,C); all three `>= 0` (boundary counts as inside); `&&` short-circuits left-to-right. Do **not** implement JS-3.0's `pointInTriangleExceptFirst`.

### 1.4 `sign`

```
int sign(double val) { return (0.0 < val) - (val < 0.0); }   // NaN -> 0
```

### 1.5 `onSegment`

```
bool onSegment(const Node* p, const Node* q, const Node* r) {   // q inside bbox(p,r)
    return q->x <= std::max<double>(p->x, r->x) &&
           q->x >= std::min<double>(p->x, r->x) &&
           q->y <= std::max<double>(p->y, r->y) &&
           q->y >= std::min<double>(p->y, r->y);
}
```

### 1.6 `intersects`

```
bool intersects(const Node* p1, const Node* q1, const Node* p2, const Node* q2) {
    int o1 = sign(area(p1, q1, p2));
    int o2 = sign(area(p1, q1, q2));
    int o3 = sign(area(p2, q2, p1));
    int o4 = sign(area(p2, q2, q1));

    if (o1 != o2 && o3 != o4) return true;                 // general case
    if (o1 == 0 && onSegment(p1, p2, q1)) return true;
    if (o2 == 0 && onSegment(p1, q2, q1)) return true;
    if (o3 == 0 && onSegment(p2, p1, q2)) return true;
    if (o4 == 0 && onSegment(p2, q1, q2)) return true;
    return false;
}
```
All four orientations computed up front (no short-circuit). This version returns **true** for touching/collinear-overlapping segments — intentional; callers filter by index.

### 1.7 `intersectsPolygon`

```
bool intersectsPolygon(const Node* a, const Node* b) {
    const Node* p = a;
    do {
        if (p->i != a->i && p->next->i != a->i && p->i != b->i && p->next->i != b->i &&
            intersects(p, p->next, a, b)) return true;
        p = p->next;
    } while (p != a);
    return false;
}
```
Forward from `a`. Guards compare `->i`, **not** pointers. `intersects` arg order is `(p, p->next, a, b)`.

### 1.8 `locallyInside`

```
bool locallyInside(const Node* a, const Node* b) {
    return area(a->prev, a, a->next) < 0 ?
        (area(a, b, a->next) >= 0 && area(a, a->prev, b) >= 0) :
        (area(a, b, a->prev) < 0  || area(a, a->next, b) < 0);
}
```
Convex branch is `>= 0 && >= 0`; reflex branch is `< 0 || < 0`. Discriminant strict `< 0` (collinear `a` → reflex branch). The asymmetry is deliberate.

### 1.9 `middleInside`

```
bool middleInside(const Node* a, const Node* b) {
    const Node* p = a;
    bool inside = false;
    double px = (a->x + b->x) / 2;
    double py = (a->y + b->y) / 2;
    do {
        if (((p->y > py) != (p->next->y > py)) && p->next->y != p->y &&
            (px < (p->next->x - p->x) * (py - p->y) / (p->next->y - p->y) + p->x))
            inside = !inside;
        p = p->next;
    } while (p != a);
    return inside;
}
```
Multiply, then divide, then add `p->x`. `/ 2` (compilers legally rewrite as `* 0.5`; bit-identical).

### 1.10 `sectorContainsSector`

```
bool sectorContainsSector(const Node* m, const Node* p) {
    return area(m->prev, m, p->prev) < 0 && area(p->next, m, m->next) < 0;
}
```
`m` is the MIDDLE argument in both; both strict `< 0`.

### 1.11 `isValidDiagonal`

```
bool isValidDiagonal(Node* a, Node* b) {
    return a->next->i != b->i && a->prev->i != b->i && !intersectsPolygon(a, b) &&
           ( ( locallyInside(a, b) && locallyInside(b, a) && middleInside(a, b) &&
               (area(a->prev, a, b->prev) != 0 || area(a, b->prev, b) != 0) )   // no opposite-facing sectors
             ||
             ( equals(a, b) && area(a->prev, a, a->next) > 0
                            && area(b->prev, b, b->next) > 0 ) );               // zero-length special case
}
```
Grouping is `A && B && C && (D || E)`. Note both sector-areas use `b->prev` (never `b->next`); both zero-length areas use strict `> 0`.

### 1.12 `insertNode`

```
Node* insertNode(std::size_t i, const Point& pt, Node* last) {
    Node* p = nodes.construct(static_cast<N>(i), nth<0>(pt), nth<1>(pt));
    if (!last) { p->prev = p; p->next = p; }
    else {
        p->next = last->next;
        p->prev = last;
        last->next->prev = p;     // must read last->next BEFORE the next line
        last->next = p;
    }
    return p;                      // returns the NEW node -> `last` walks forward
}
```

### 1.13 `removeNode`

```
void removeNode(Node* p) {
    p->next->prev = p->prev;
    p->prev->next = p->next;
    if (p->prevZ) p->prevZ->nextZ = p->nextZ;
    if (p->nextZ) p->nextZ->prevZ = p->prevZ;
}
```
Does **not** clear `p`'s own pointers and does **not** free `p`. Callers read through the removed node afterwards (`filterPoints`: `p = end = p->prev`; `cureLocalIntersections`: `removeNode(p->next)` after `removeNode(p)`). Do not add cleanup.

### 1.14 `filterPoints`

```
Node* filterPoints(Node* start, Node* end = nullptr) {
    if (!end) end = start;
    Node* p = start;
    bool again;
    do {
        again = false;
        if (!p->steiner && (equals(p, p->next) || area(p->prev, p, p->next) == 0)) {
            removeNode(p);
            p = end = p->prev;
            if (p == p->next) break;
            again = true;
        } else {
            p = p->next;
        }
    } while (again || p != end);
    return end;
}
```
`area(...) == 0` exact. Steiner nodes immune. **Returns `end`**, which is algorithm state, not a convenience — callers reassign their anchor from it. A leading `if (!start) return start;` may or may not be in the file; it cannot change output (see Switch #13).

### 1.15 `splitPolygon`

```
Node* splitPolygon(Node* a, Node* b) {
    Node* a2 = nodes.construct(a->i, a->x, a->y);   // a2 constructed FIRST
    Node* b2 = nodes.construct(b->i, b->x, b->y);
    Node* an = a->next;                              // captured BEFORE relinking
    Node* bp = b->prev;

    a->next  = b;   b->prev  = a;
    a2->next = an;  an->prev = a2;
    b2->next = a2;  a2->prev = b2;
    bp->next = b2;  b2->prev = bp;

    return b2;
}
```
`a2`/`b2` duplicate `i` (that is why every neighbour test compares `->i`), and start with `z=0, prevZ=nextZ=nullptr, steiner=false` regardless of the originals — so they are **not** in the z-chain until a later `indexCurve` adopts them.

### 1.16 `linkedList`

```
Node* linkedList(const Ring& points, const bool clockwise) {
    double sum = 0;
    const std::size_t len = points.size();
    std::size_t i, j;
    Node* last = nullptr;

    for (i = 0, j = (len > 0 ? len - 1 : 0); i < len; j = i++) {
        const auto& p1 = points[i];      // CURRENT
        const auto& p2 = points[j];      // PREVIOUS
        const double p20 = nth<0>(p2), p10 = nth<0>(p1);
        const double p11 = nth<1>(p1), p21 = nth<1>(p2);
        sum += (p20 - p10) * (p11 + p21);
    }

    if (clockwise == (sum > 0)) {
        for (i = 0; i < len; i++)   last = insertNode(vertices + i, points[i], last);
    } else {
        for (i = len; i-- > 0;)     last = insertNode(vertices + i, points[i], last);
    }

    if (last && equals(last, last->next)) { removeNode(last); last = last->next; }

    vertices += len;
    return last;                          // the LAST-INSERTED node
}
```
* Index is always `vertices + (position in the input ring)`, **even in the reverse loop** → output indices are flat-input offsets.
* `vertices += len` adds the full `len` even if the trailing duplicate was dropped.
* `clockwise == (sum > 0)`: strict `>`, so a zero-area ring is "not clockwise".
* Returned anchor: `points[len-1]`'s node in the forward case, `points[0]`'s node in the reverse case (possibly shifted by the dedup). **This anchor is the traversal start for everything downstream.**
* Called `linkedList(points[0], true)` for the outer ring, `linkedList(points[i], false)` for holes.
* For the square input: `sum = 2n² > 0` for every ring ⇒ outer inserts **forward** (anchor = last corner), holes insert **reversed** (anchor = the `(2i,2i)` corner).

### 1.17 `getLeftmost`

```
Node* getLeftmost(Node* start) {
    Node* p = start;
    Node* leftmost = start;
    do {
        if (p->x < leftmost->x || (p->x == leftmost->x && p->y < leftmost->y))
            leftmost = p;
        p = p->next;
    } while (p != start);
    return leftmost;
}
```
Key = (min x, then min y); both strict `<`, so an exact duplicate loses to the earlier-visited node.

### 1.18 `findHoleBridge`

```
Node* findHoleBridge(Node* hole, Node* outerNode) {
    Node*  p  = outerNode;
    double hx = hole->x;
    double hy = hole->y;
    double qx = -infinity;            // literal -inf, not -DBL_MAX
    Node*  m  = nullptr;

    // ---- loop 1: leftward ray cast ----
    do {
        if (hy <= p->y && hy >= p->next->y && p->next->y != p->y) {
            double x = p->x + (hy - p->y) * (p->next->x - p->x) / (p->next->y - p->y);
            if (x <= hx && x > qx) {
                qx = x;
                m  = (p->x < p->next->x) ? p : p->next;
                if (x == hx) return m;        // hole touches outer segment: EARLY RETURN
            }
        }
        p = p->next;
    } while (p != outerNode);

    if (!m) return nullptr;

    // ---- loop 2: minimum-tangent selection ----
    const Node* stop = m;                 // SNAPSHOT of the initial m
    double tanMin = +infinity;
    double mx = m->x;                     // SNAPSHOT, never updated when m moves
    double my = m->y;

    p = m;                                // starts AT m (do/while), so m itself is tested
    do {
        if (hx >= p->x && p->x >= mx && hx != p->x &&
            pointInTriangle(hy < my ? hx : qx, hy,
                            mx,                my,
                            hy < my ? qx : hx, hy,
                            p->x,              p->y)) {

            double tan = std::abs(hy - p->y) / (hx - p->x);

            if (locallyInside(p, hole) &&
                (tan < tanMin || (tan == tanMin && (p->x > m->x || sectorContainsSector(m, p))))) {
                m = p;
                tanMin = tan;
            }
        }
        p = p->next;
    } while (p != stop);

    return m;
}
```
Load-bearing details:
* Loop 1 traverses **forward from `outerNode`**, testing `outerNode` first. Only downward edges qualify (`<=` on the from-endpoint, `>=` on the to-endpoint). `x > qx` is **strict**, so among segments producing the identical `x`, the **first reached from the anchor** wins → the anchor identity is observable.
* Ray x expression: multiply, then divide, then add `p->x`.
* `m = p->x < p->next->x ? p : p->next` — strict `<`; vertical edge (tie) picks `p->next`.
* `if (x == hx) return m;` sits **inside** the accept block, **after** `qx = x` and **after** `m` is assigned. There is **no** post-loop `if (hx == qx) return m->prev;` in 2.2.4.
* Loop 2 gate uses the **frozen** `mx`; the tie-break uses the **live** `m->x`. Replicate both.
* `locallyInside(p, hole)` is evaluated **first** in 2.2.x (cost-only difference).
* The `sectorContainsSector` arm is **UNGATED** in the C++ port (no `p->x == m->x &&` prefix). JS has the gate; C++ does not.

### 1.19 `eliminateHole`  — 2.2.3/2.2.4 form (V3)

```
Node* eliminateHole(Node* hole, Node* outerNode) {
    Node* bridge = findHoleBridge(hole, outerNode);
    if (!bridge) return outerNode;

    Node* bridgeReverse = splitPolygon(bridge, hole);   // arg order: (outer node, hole node)

    // filter collinear points around the cuts
    filterPoints(bridgeReverse, bridgeReverse->next);   // bridgeReverse FIRST
    // Check if input node was removed by the filtering
    return filterPoints(bridge, bridge->next);          // return value = new outer anchor
}
```

### 1.20 `eliminateHoles`

```
Node* eliminateHoles(const Polygon& points, Node* outerNode) {
    const size_t len = points.size();

    queue.clear();
    for (size_t i = 1; i < len; i++) {                 // ASCENDING ring order
        Node* list = linkedList(points[i], false);     // clockwise = false
        if (list) {
            if (list == list->next) list->steiner = true;   // 1-node hole => Steiner
            queue.push_back(getLeftmost(list));
        }
    }

    std::sort(queue.begin(), queue.end(),
              [](const Node* a, const Node* b) { return a->x < b->x; });   // x ONLY

    for (size_t i = 0; i < queue.size(); i++)
        outerNode = eliminateHole(queue[i], outerNode);

    return outerNode;
}
```
All holes are linked (and thus numbered) **before** the sort, so node indices are unaffected by hole ordering. There is **no** second `filterPoints` in this loop (that is the pre-2.2.3 form).

### 1.21 `zOrder`

```
int32_t zOrder(const double x_, const double y_) {
    int32_t x = static_cast<int32_t>((x_ - minX) * inv_size);   // MULTIPLY by reciprocal
    int32_t y = static_cast<int32_t>((y_ - minY) * inv_size);

    x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
    x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
    x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
    x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;

    y = (y | (y << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
    y = (y | (y << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
    y = (y | (y << 2)) & 0x33333333;
    y = (y | (y << 1)) & 0x55555555;

    return x | (y << 1);
}
```
Truncating cast (toward zero, == JS `|0`). The `32767.` constant lives in `operator()`, **not** here. Both axes share one isotropic `inv_size`.

### 1.22 `sortLinked` (Simon Tatham bottom-up merge sort on the z-chain)

```
Node* sortLinked(Node* list) {
    Node *p, *q, *e, *tail;
    int i, numMerges, pSize, qSize;
    int inSize = 1;

    for (;;) {
        p = list; list = nullptr; tail = nullptr; numMerges = 0;

        while (p) {
            numMerges++;
            q = p; pSize = 0;
            for (i = 0; i < inSize; i++) { pSize++; q = q->nextZ; if (!q) break; }
            qSize = inSize;

            while (pSize > 0 || (qSize > 0 && q)) {
                if      (pSize == 0)          { e = q; q = q->nextZ; qSize--; }
                else if (qSize == 0 || !q)    { e = p; p = p->nextZ; pSize--; }
                else if (p->z <= q->z)        { e = p; p = p->nextZ; pSize--; }
                else                          { e = q; q = q->nextZ; qSize--; }

                if (tail) tail->nextZ = e; else list = e;
                e->prevZ = tail;
                tail = e;
            }
            p = q;
        }
        tail->nextZ = nullptr;
        if (numMerges <= 1) return list;
        inSize *= 2;
    }
}
```
`p->z <= q->z` makes it **stable**; z-ties are common (duplicate coords, same 15-bit cell), so stability is load-bearing.

### 1.23 `indexCurve`

```
void indexCurve(Node* start) {
    assert(start);
    Node* p = start;
    do {
        p->z = p->z ? p->z : zOrder(p->x, p->y);   // reuse already-computed z
        p->prevZ = p->prev;
        p->nextZ = p->next;
        p = p->next;
    } while (p != start);

    p->prevZ->nextZ = nullptr;    // p == start here: cut the circle
    p->prevZ = nullptr;

    sortLinked(p);                // return value DELIBERATELY DISCARDED
}
```
Pre-sort sequence is `start, start->next, ..., start->prev` — combined with the stable sort, **the identity of `start` determines the post-sort order of equal-z nodes**, hence the search order in `isEarHashed`.

### 1.24 `isEar`

```
bool isEar(Node* ear) {
    const Node* a = ear->prev;
    const Node* b = ear;
    const Node* c = ear->next;

    if (area(a, b, c) >= 0) return false;      // reflex, can't be an ear

    Node* p = ear->next->next;
    while (p != ear->prev) {
        if (pointInTriangle(a->x, a->y, b->x, b->y, c->x, c->y, p->x, p->y) &&
            area(p->prev, p, p->next) >= 0) return false;
        p = p->next;
    }
    return true;
}
```
Scan is exactly the strictly-interior vertices: from `ear->next->next` forward, stopping at `ear->prev`. Both rejections use `>= 0`. Convex intruders are ignored — only reflex/collinear intruders veto. (No coordinate bbox pre-filter; see Switch #12 — adding one is output-neutral.)

### 1.25 `isEarHashed`

```
bool isEarHashed(Node* ear) {
    const Node* a = ear->prev;
    const Node* b = ear;
    const Node* c = ear->next;

    if (area(a, b, c) >= 0) return false;

    // triangle bbox; min & max are calculated like this for speed
    const double minTX = std::min<double>(a->x, std::min<double>(b->x, c->x));
    const double minTY = std::min<double>(a->y, std::min<double>(b->y, c->y));
    const double maxTX = std::max<double>(a->x, std::max<double>(b->x, c->x));
    const double maxTY = std::max<double>(a->y, std::max<double>(b->y, c->y));

    const int32_t minZ = zOrder(minTX, minTY);      // computed FIRST
    const int32_t maxZ = zOrder(maxTX, maxTY);      // computed SECOND

    Node* p = ear->prevZ;
    Node* n = ear->nextZ;

    while (p && p->z >= minZ && n && n->z <= maxZ) {
        if (p->x >= minTX && p->x <= maxTX && p->y >= minTY && p->y <= maxTY &&
            p != ear->prev && p != ear->next &&
            pointInTriangle(a->x,a->y,b->x,b->y,c->x,c->y,p->x,p->y) &&
            area(p->prev, p, p->next) >= 0) return false;
        p = p->prevZ;

        if (n->x >= minTX && n->x <= maxTX && n->y >= minTY && n->y <= maxTY &&
            n != ear->prev && n != ear->next &&
            pointInTriangle(a->x,a->y,b->x,b->y,c->x,c->y,n->x,n->y) &&
            area(n->prev, n, n->next) >= 0) return false;
        n = n->nextZ;
    }

    while (p && p->z >= minZ) {          // drain prevZ side
        if (p != ear->prev && p != ear->next &&
            p->x >= minTX && p->x <= maxTX && p->y >= minTY && p->y <= maxTY &&
            pointInTriangle(a->x,a->y,b->x,b->y,c->x,c->y,p->x,p->y) &&
            area(p->prev, p, p->next) >= 0) return false;
        p = p->prevZ;
    }

    while (n && n->z <= maxZ) {          // drain nextZ side
        if (n != ear->prev && n != ear->next &&
            n->x >= minTX && n->x <= maxTX && n->y >= minTY && n->y <= maxTY &&
            pointInTriangle(a->x,a->y,b->x,b->y,c->x,c->y,n->x,n->y) &&
            area(n->prev, n, n->next) >= 0) return false;
        n = n->nextZ;
    }

    return true;
}
```
**Both z bounds inclusive.** The candidate SET scanned (all `prevZ` nodes with `z >= minZ` ∪ all `nextZ` nodes with `z <= maxZ`) is identical to the older two-sequential-loops form, and the result is an OR over that set, so the two shapes are **output-identical** (Disagreement D3). The coordinate-bbox conjunct is a conservative pre-filter and is likewise output-neutral. Keep the `p != ear->prev && p != ear->next` guards (JS 3.0 drops them — do not).

### 1.26 `cureLocalIntersections`

```
Node* cureLocalIntersections(Node* start) {
    Node* p = start;
    do {
        Node* a = p->prev;
        Node* b = p->next->next;

        if (!equals(a, b) && intersects(a, p, p->next, b) &&
            locallyInside(a, b) && locallyInside(b, a)) {

            indices.emplace_back(a->i);
            indices.emplace_back(p->i);
            indices.emplace_back(b->i);      // p->next is NEVER emitted, only deleted

            removeNode(p);
            removeNode(p->next);             // p->next re-read AFTER removeNode(p);
                                             //   do NOT hoist a `Node* pn = p->next;`
            p = start = b;
        }
        p = p->next;
    } while (p != start);

    return filterPoints(p);                  // p == start at exit; equivalent either way
}
```
Guard order exactly: `!equals(a,b)` → `intersects(a, p, p->next, b)` → `locallyInside(a,b)` → `locallyInside(b,a)`. Note the first `intersects` argument is `a = p->prev`, not `p`.

### 1.27 `splitEarcut`

```
void splitEarcut(Node* start) {
    Node* a = start;
    do {
        Node* b = a->next->next;
        while (b != a->prev) {
            if (a->i != b->i && isValidDiagonal(a, b)) {
                Node* c = splitPolygon(a, b);

                a = filterPoints(a, a->next);      // two-arg form
                c = filterPoints(c, c->next);

                earcutLinked(a);                   // a-half FIRST, pass defaults to 0
                earcutLinked(c);                   // then c-half
                return;                            // first valid diagonal wins; no retry
            }
            b = b->next;
        }
        a = a->next;
    } while (a != start);
}
```
Both loops forward; inner starts at `a->next->next` and stops before `a->prev`. Cheap `a->i != b->i` first. Silent give-up if no diagonal exists (those vertices are simply never triangulated). Because both recursive calls use pass 0, `indexCurve` re-runs on each half when hashing is on.

### 1.28 `earcutLinked`

```
void earcutLinked(Node* ear, int pass = 0) {
    if (!ear) return;

    if (!pass && hashing) indexCurve(ear);

    Node* stop = ear;
    Node* prev;
    Node* next;

    while (ear->prev != ear->next) {
        prev = ear->prev;
        next = ear->next;                        // both latched BEFORE the ear test

        if (hashing ? isEarHashed(ear) : isEar(ear)) {
            indices.emplace_back(prev->i);       // 1st
            indices.emplace_back(ear->i);        // 2nd
            indices.emplace_back(next->i);       // 3rd

            removeNode(ear);

            // skipping the next vertex leads to less sliver triangles
            ear  = next->next;
            stop = next->next;
            continue;
        }

        ear = next;

        if (ear == stop) {
            if      (!pass)      earcutLinked(filterPoints(ear), 1);
            else if (pass == 1) { ear = cureLocalIntersections(filterPoints(ear));
                                  earcutLinked(ear, 2); }
            else if (pass == 2)  splitEarcut(ear);
            break;                               // ALWAYS breaks, whichever branch ran
        }
    }
}
```
* Pass 0 fallback passes `filterPoints(ear)` **directly** as the argument (no intermediate assignment to `ear`). Pass 1 **does** assign before recursing.
* `hashing` (not `pass`) selects the ear test, so passes 1 and 2 still use `isEarHashed`.
* No iteration counter / bailout anywhere.

### 1.29 `operator()`

```
void operator()(const Polygon& points) {
    indices.clear();
    vertices = 0;
    if (!points.size()) return;

    double x, y;
    int threshold = 80;
    std::size_t len = 0;

    for (size_t i = 0; threshold >= 0 && i < points.size(); i++) {
        threshold -= static_cast<int>(points[i].size());
        len += points[i].size();                  // deliberately under-counts (loop short-circuits)
    }

    nodes.reset(len * 3 / 2);                     // allocation only; no output effect
    indices.reserve(len + points[0].size());      // allocation only

    Node* outerNode = linkedList(points[0], true);
    if (!outerNode || outerNode->prev == outerNode->next) return;
    // NOTE: no filterPoints on the outer ring here.

    if (points.size() > 1) outerNode = eliminateHoles(points, outerNode);

    hashing = threshold < 0;                      // strictly MORE than 80 total verts
    if (hashing) {
        Node* p = outerNode->next;                // walks the MERGED ring (holes included!)
        minX = maxX = outerNode->x;
        minY = maxY = outerNode->y;
        do {
            x = p->x; y = p->y;
            minX = std::min<double>(minX, x);
            minY = std::min<double>(minY, y);
            maxX = std::max<double>(maxX, x);
            maxY = std::max<double>(maxY, y);
            p = p->next;
        } while (p != outerNode);

        inv_size = std::max<double>(maxX - minX, maxY - minY);
        inv_size = inv_size != .0 ? (32767. / inv_size) : .0;   // two statements, one variable
    }

    earcutLinked(outerNode);                      // pass = 0

    nodes.clear();
}
```
The bbox is computed **after** `eliminateHoles`, over the merged linked list — a genuine C++-vs-JS divergence (JS uses the raw outer-ring slice). Do not "fix" it.

